\section{Scope and attribution}
Throughout, graphs are finite, simple and connected when called targets. Fix
integers $n\geq3$, $k\geq2$, $g\geq3$ and $\lambda\geq1$. A target is a
$k$-regular graph on $n$ vertices of girth $g$ in which every edge lies on
exactly $\lambda$ cycles of length $g$. Cycles are unoriented.

The fixed source is Goedgebeur and Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of
edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024. Its Section 2
supplies shortest-path counting; its Lemma 1 and Section 3 supply the Moore
seed, horizontal quotas and generation design. The source attributes the
seed lemma to Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman, Theorem 2.3. We prove
these elementary interfaces below rather than retaining an unproved seed
claim. The chronological coverage argument is acquired from the complete
F009 manuscript \emph{Chronological coverage without a saturated current
vertex} and its distinct review. Its original mathematical contributors
were the root agent and enumeration\_volume. We adapt it here to an
unrooted target and a corrected first phase, giving the full argument.

Printed Algorithm 2 requires a correction. The explicit counterexample in
Section 3 was supplied by the root agent and independently checked by
publication\_recovery. The latter authors the present proof and reference
implementation; a separate review is required. No numerical table of the
source is inferred from this algorithmic theorem, and no historical
exhaustion is certified merely by proving an algorithm correct.

\section{Counting cycles and embedding the seed}
For a partial graph $H$ of girth at least $g$ and $e=uv\in E(H)$, let
$c_g(H,e)$ count its $g$-cycles through $e$.
\begin{lemma}\label{count}
Delete $e$ and perform breadth-first search from $u$. Set $a(u)=1$ and,
for every other reached vertex $x$, set
\[
 a(x)=\sum_{y\sim x,\ d(u,y)=d(u,x)-1}a(y).
\]
Then $c_g(H,e)=a(v)$ if $d(u,v)=g-1$, and is zero otherwise. In the
unreachable case it is zero. This uses $O(|V(H)|+|E(H)|)$ arithmetic
operations.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Deleting $uv$ gives a bijection between cycles through this edge and
simple $u$--$v$ paths of length one less. No such path has length below
$g-1$. Thus paths of length $g-1$, when present, are shortest paths.
Every shortest path to $x$ has a unique penultimate vertex in the preceding
breadth-first layer; conversely each summand extends a shortest path
uniquely. This proves the recurrence by induction on the distance.
Before $v$ is removed from the FIFO queue, every vertex in its preceding
layer has been processed. Returning at that point, as in source Algorithm
1, therefore uses its final count. Each adjacency is examined at most
twice and each vertex is queued once.
\end{proof}
The complexity is an arithmetic-operation statement. If counts have $B$
bits, ordinary exact additions give $O((|V|+|E|)B)$ bit operations.
For the decision whether the count is less than, equal to, or greater than
$\lambda$, every addition may instead be capped at $\lambda+1$:
$\min(C,a+b)=\min(C,\min(C,a)+\min(C,b))$ for $C=\lambda+1$.
This yields $B=O(\log(\lambda+2))$ and does not assume machine integers
cannot overflow. Only layers through $g-1$ need be expanded.

For odd $g=2s+1$, let $T$ be the tree rooted at a vertex $o$, of depth
$s$, with every internal vertex of degree $k$. Its $k$ leaf groups $L_i$
correspond to the children of $o$. For even $g=2s$, let $T$ consist of an
edge $o_1o_2$ and two rooted trees of depth $s-1$ attached at its ends,
again with internal degree $k$; its two leaf groups are the two sides.
Call an edge between distinct groups horizontal. Write $h_i(H)$ for the
number of horizontal edges of $H$ incident with $L_i$.
\begin{lemma}\label{seed}
Every target contains $T$. For every such embedded copy,
$h_i(H)=\lambda$ in the target, for every group $i$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expose neighbors from the root, or from both ends of the central edge,
until the prescribed depth. A collision between two exposed tree paths
would create a cycle of length at most $2s$ in the odd case, or $2s-1$
in the even case. These lengths are below $g$. Thus all required new
vertices are distinct and every internal vertex has all its $k$ neighbors
in the tree.

In the odd case, a horizontal edge incident with $L_i$, together with the
unique tree path between its ends, gives a $g$-cycle through the root edge
leading to $L_i$. Conversely a $g$-cycle through that root edge consists
of two length-$s$ paths from $o$, in different branches, and the joining
edge. Each path is in the exposed tree: all its internal vertices lie at
distance below $s$ and already have their full neighbor sets there.
Equivalently, the uniqueness of paths of length at most $s$ from $o$
follows from girth greater than $2s$. This proves the claimed bijection.
In the even case apply the same argument to a cycle through $o_1o_2$:
the remaining path has two initial segments of length $s-1$ in the two
trees and a joining edge. Uniqueness follows from the collision bound
$2s-1$. Both group counts equal the number of these central-edge cycles.
An edge between two leaves in the same group would give a cycle of
length at most $2s-1$ in the odd case or $2s-1$ in the even case,
and is forbidden.
\end{proof}

\section{The printed rule and its correction}
Take $(n,k,g,\lambda)=(9,4,3,1)$ and the seed star with center $0$,
leaves $1,2,3,4$, and isolated vertices $5,6,7,8$. After adding the first
horizontal edge, say $12$, root edges $01,02$ have their one triangle,
whereas $03,04$ have none. Hence the first phase is incomplete.
Each of leaves $1,2$ has exactly four valid neighbors, namely the isolated
vertices. Leaves $3,4$ each have five, namely the other remaining leaf
and the four isolates. Printed Algorithm 2, line 19, must choose $1$ or
$2$. Its line 22 then leaves an empty horizontal-edge loop. The call
returns without adding $34$. Every vertex still passes the degree-capacity
test. Earlier first-edge attempts are incident with the initial selected
leaf; after the chosen edge they are already invalid, so historical
exclusions do not change this argument. Relabeling covers all first
choices and tie orders.

There is a target: the $3$ by $3$ rook graph, with adjacency given by
agreement in one coordinate. It is connected and $4$-regular, and each
edge has exactly one common neighbor. The independent certificate checks
all $4\cdot6\cdot3=72$ ordered first-branch histories and the six literal
triangles of this graph. The counterexample is to the printed rule only.

The corrected first phase chooses a group $L_i$ with $h_i(H)<\lambda$
and tries \emph{every} eligible horizontal edge incident with that group,
not merely the edges at one arbitrarily chosen leaf. The order may be
organized by vertices with fewest options, provided the loop continues
through the group. Once every quota is met, the second phase starts.
The later source implementation at commit
\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2} (December 2024)
uses such a deficient-group loop; it is not the printed June algorithm.

\section{A precise generation interface}
Start from $T$ and $n-|V(T)|$ isolates. Return immediately if the seed
has too many vertices. Maintain a graph $H$ of maximum degree $k$ and
girth at least $g$, together with a set $F$ of historically excluded
edges. An edge is necessary-admissible if it is absent, its ends have
degree below $k$, its addition preserves girth at least $g$, and no
edge after its addition has more than $\lambda$ $g$-cycles.
One may omit the final test; the final output test must remain exact.
Eligible edges are necessary-admissible edges outside $F$.

Execute a serial depth-first recursion with the following rules.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Compare an exact uncolored isomorphism key for the entire graph on
$n$ vertices with previously entered states. On a hit return. Otherwise
store the key, or omit storage when memory is full. All stored terminal
keys must be retained if duplicate-free output is required.
\item If $H$ is $k$-regular, output it precisely when it is connected and
each edge has count $\lambda$ at length $g$, and return.
\item If some vertex has degree plus eligible-neighbor count below $k$,
return. In the first phase select a deficient group and list all eligible
horizontal edges incident with it. Edges exceeding a group quota are
omitted. In the second phase choose an unsaturated nonisolated vertex
and list all eligible incident edges; horizontal edges may be omitted
when their quotas are full. If no such vertex exists while isolates
remain, return, since no connected completion is possible.
\item For a nonisolated current vertex, keep only the edge to the least
isolated vertex among all isolated-neighbor possibilities. This choice
is made on the graph's isolated set, before historical exclusions; if
that representative edge is excluded, the coverage argument below
applies. First-phase edges have no isolated endpoint.
\item In any fixed order, attempt each listed edge $e$: add it, recompute
necessary restrictions, and recurse if the necessary tests permit.
After the attempt finishes, restore the parent graph and its records,
then put $e$ in the parent's $F$. Each child inherits the exclusions
already present when it starts. Never install an exclusion on account
of a running or interrupted attempt.
\end{enumerate}
All state restoration is exact. A child receives a copy of the parent's
historical set, so exclusions discovered inside that child do not leak
into a sibling except for the parent's own attempted edge. This is the
interface used by the reference implementation.

\begin{theorem}\label{coverage}
An uninterrupted execution of this interface outputs exactly all target
isomorphism classes. With retained exact terminal keys it outputs each
class once. Omitting the first phase, or omitting excess-cycle tests
until the terminal test, preserves this conclusion.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every output has the required degree and cycle counts by the terminal
test. Girth is at least $g$ throughout; since $\lambda>0$ and an edge is
present, it is exactly $g$. Connectedness is tested explicitly.

Call a partial graph covered at a time if every target completion of it
has a class already output. Coverage persists with time and passes to
supergraphs. We induct over the chronological sequence of finished
attempts and recursive returns, as in the acquired F009 argument. An
edge historically excluded at $H$ was previously attempted at a graph
$K$ with $K\subseteq H$. That attempt has finished. Thus all completions
of $H+e$ are already represented by the induction hypothesis. A degree
shortage can therefore mean that completions exist but are already
represented; it need not prove that no completion exists.

A repeated isomorphism key belongs to an earlier state with the same
number of edges. Strict edge growth rules out an ancestor, so in a
serial depth-first traversal that state has finished. Relabeling transfers
any completion of the present graph to a target completion of that
state. No marked root is part of the target property. The earlier state
contains its own embedded seed, and Lemma~\ref{seed} applies to every
such embedding. Thus a key need not preserve a chosen root, seed,
quota vector or historical exclusion set.

Suppose that a nonterminal return leaves an unrepresented class. Choose
one completion $G$ in that class. It uses no necessary-forbidden edge,
and it avoids every historical exclusion by the preceding argument.
In a deficient group, Lemma~\ref{seed} supplies a missing horizontal
edge of $G$ incident with that group. Its ends are unsaturated in $H$
and neither incident quota can be full. Hence the first-phase list
contains a required edge, unless its attempt has already finished,
which would already represent the class. In the second phase, any
chosen unsaturated vertex has a missing neighbor in $G$. If all existing
nonisolated vertices were saturated while isolates remained, their
nonempty component would be closed in $G$, contrary to connectedness.
Full horizontal quotas preclude any further horizontal edge of $G$.

When a required neighbor is isolated, exchange isolated labels to move
it to the least isolate. This fixes $H$, the nonisolated current vertex
and the embedded seed, and preserves the target class. If the representative
edge is historically excluded, its transformed completion is already
represented. A necessary exclusion is impossible for a target completion.
Otherwise its branch is attempted. Asymmetry of historical masks does
not affect this reasoning.

The chosen attempt cannot fail a necessary test. A capacity or visited
return is covered by the arguments above; otherwise its recursive
continuation finishes before the present return and is covered by
induction. In every case the class was already represented, a
contradiction. This proves coverage of each return and of each finished
attempt. The root return covers every target by Lemma~\ref{seed}.
Removing a necessary test only enlarges the attempted set; removing
the first phase uses the already proved second-phase argument from the
seed onward. Exact terminal keys reject later isomorphic outputs.
\end{proof}
The recursion is finite: each step adds an edge, with at most $nk/2$
edges in a state, and each finite branch list is attempted once. A time
limit, unfinished split, arithmetic failure or allocation failure is not
a root return and proves no exhaustion statement.

\section{Relation to the implementation and to numerical evidence}
The attached interface ledger pins the later C file, source edition and
actual procedure ranges. Its \texttt{calcNumberShortestPaths} implements
the recurrence of Lemma~\ref{count}, deleting and then restoring the
edge. The deficient-group outer loop supplies the corrected first phase;
a group vertex is skipped only after all its available horizontal attempts
have finished. Such a skip, when inherited, represents coverage of
completions using its previously attempted edges, rather than an assertion
that the vertex has no missing edges in any completion.

The C implementation maintains distances incrementally. For an added
edge $ab$, the new distance from $x$ to $y$ is the minimum of the old
distance and the two quantities $d'(x,a)+1+d'(b,y)$ and
$d'(x,b)+1+d'(a,y)$, where $d'$ is computed in the new graph. Each
quantity is a walk length and cannot underestimate distance. If a new
shortest path uses $ab$, its two subpaths show one quantity is at most
its length, proving equality. Distances only need be maintained for
currently available pairs; a pair deleted in a branch cannot return until
its ancestor snapshot is restored. The two snapshot levels in the code
separate branch restoration from parent historical exclusions.

These observations identify the mathematical rules, not a general
machine-code correctness theorem. The historical C program uses signed
\texttt{int} path counts and fixed arrays; all additions, dimensions,
seed allocation and bit shifts require bounds. Its canonical routine
allocates and compares variable-length arrays on the active vertices:
an exact-key contract must include the active count and valid comparison
length, or use the fixed $n$-vertex key of our interface. The supplied
reference uses exact Python integers and fixed-size graph keys. We do
not alter the historical file, assume the missing machine premises,
or certify a six-CPU-year run from small tests.

The checker independently enumerates small labelled graphs, counts simple
cycles directly, and compares complete small outputs with both one-phase
and corrected two-phase reference searches. It also checks the printed
counterexample independently. These controls exercise the interface;
the uniform completeness assertion is Theorem~\ref{coverage}. Source
numerical minima, uniqueness assertions and enumeration totals each
still require their own completed evidence or a separate proof.
